<p id="S2.F2.4.1" class="ltx_p ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:14.9pt;"><span id="S2.F2.4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">(b)</span><span id="S2.F2.4.1.2" class="ltx_text"></span></p>
<div id="S2.F2.4.2" class="ltx_block ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:482.0pt;"><img src="2507.06510v1/gen-vlkt_attn.png" id="S2.F2.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:665/99;" width="665" height="99" alt="Refer to caption">
</div>
</div></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S2.F2.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Visualization of attention maps. (a) Self-attention maps for the <math id="S2.F2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="cls\_token" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">cls\_token</annotation></semantics></math> of the ViT encoder of BLIP-2. These maps are extracted from the shallow (left) to deep (right) layers. It is shown that the attention of the <math id="S2.F2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="cls\_token" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">cls\_token</annotation></semantics></math> tends to spread across the entire image. (b) Cross-attention maps for high-score HOI queries in the HOI detector’s interaction decoder. It is shown that each query focuses on local areas of one specific human-object pair.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Method</h2>

<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2507.06510v1/drawing_1.png" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:685/300;" width="685" height="300" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F3.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S3.F3.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Overview of our bilateral collaboration framework between the HOI detector and VLM. The collaborations are reflected in three aspects: (1) The early fusion between the visual features extracted by the BLIP-2 Vision Tower and the CNN backbone of the HOI detector. (2) The HOI detector provides attention bias to BLIP-2, enabling its Vision Tower to extract fine-grained instance-level interaction features. (3) The BLIP-2 model utilizes its LLM component (OPT-2.7B) to impose fine-grained supervision on the HOI detector. The <span id="S3.F3.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#9AC6F8;">Blue</span> shadow represents the BLIP-2 model, while the <span id="S3.F3.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFF5C8;">yellow</span> one represents the HOI detector. The LLM is abandoned during the inference stage.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we explain our bilateral collaboration framework between the HOI detector and VLM in detail. We first introduce the overview of our framework in Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Overview ‣ 3 Method ‣ Bilateral Collaboration with Large Vision-Language Models forOpen Vocabulary Human-Object Interaction Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>. Then, we present the guidance from the HOI Detector to VLM in Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Guidance from the HOI Detector to VLM ‣ 3 Method ‣ Bilateral Collaboration with Large Vision-Language Models forOpen Vocabulary Human-Object Interaction Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> and the guidance from VLM to the HOI Detector in Section <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Guidance from VLM to the HOI Detector ‣ 3 Method ‣ Bilateral Collaboration with Large Vision-Language Models forOpen Vocabulary Human-Object Interaction Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>, respectively.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Overview</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Similar to existing works for open vocabulary HOI detection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, our framework includes one HOI detector and one VLM. We adopt the same HOI detector as <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>. Specifically, the HOI detector includes one CNN backbone, one transformer encoder <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib81" title="" class="ltx_ref">81</a>]</cite>, one detection decoder, and one interaction decoder. The detection decoder has two groups of learnable queries as input (i.e., <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{Q}_{\text{h}}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{\text{q}}\times D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑸</mi><mtext>h</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>q</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{Q}_{\text{h}}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{\text{q}}\times D}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{Q}_{\text{o}}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{\text{q}}\times D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑸</mi><mtext>o</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>q</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{Q}_{\text{o}}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{\text{q}}\times D}</annotation></semantics></math>) for human and object detection, respectively, as shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3 Method ‣ Bilateral Collaboration with Large Vision-Language Models forOpen Vocabulary Human-Object Interaction Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\text{q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>q</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\text{q}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> denote the query number and embedding dimension, respectively. The output embeddings of the detection decoder are denoted as <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{E}_{\text{h}}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{\text{q}}\times D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑬</mi><mtext>h</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>q</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{E}_{\text{h}}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{\text{q}}\times D}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{E}_{\text{o}}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{\text{q}}\times D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑬</mi><mtext>o</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>q</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{E}_{\text{o}}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{\text{q}}\times D}</annotation></semantics></math>, which are first fused via element-wise addition and then utilized as the queries for the interaction decoder. Moreover, as illustrated in Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3 Method ‣ Bilateral Collaboration with Large Vision-Language Models forOpen Vocabulary Human-Object Interaction Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we adopt BLIP-2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite> as the VLM following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, which includes one ViT encoder <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">73</a>]</cite>, one Q-Former <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>, and one LLM model named OPT-2.7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">75</a>]</cite>. For simplicity, we refer to the cascading ViT encoder and Q-Former as the Vision Tower of BLIP-2, which functions as a visual encoder.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Existing one-stage methods usually adopt late fusion for the features extracted by the HOI detector and VLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>. Specifically, they employ the interaction decoder and HOI queries to extract representations from feature maps output by the backbone of the HOI detector and the visual encoder of VLM, respectively. The obtained interaction representations are fused via element-wise addition and used for interaction classification purposes, as illustrated in Figure <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Abstract ‣ Bilateral Collaboration with Large Vision-Language Models forOpen Vocabulary Human-Object Interaction Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(a).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In comparison, we introduce the early fusion strategy. Specifically, we first concatenate the feature maps produced by the CNN backbone and the BLIP-2 Vision Tower along the spatial dimension. Then, we adopt the transformer encoder to refine the concatenated feature maps. The refined feature map is denoted as <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{M}\in\mathbb{R}^{(h\times w+N_{\text{p}})\times D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑴</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>p</mtext></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{M}\in\mathbb{R}^{(h\times w+N_{\text{p}})\times D}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> represent the height and width of the CNN feature maps, and <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\text{p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>p</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\text{p}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the number of patch tokens in BLIP-2. Compared to the late fusion approach, the early fusion strategy can capture more fine-grained visual feature correlations between the two backbones. Then, both the detection and interaction decoders perform cross-attention on <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{M}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In the Section <a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4 Ablation Studies ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Bilateral Collaboration with Large Vision-Language Models forOpen Vocabulary Human-Object Interaction Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>, we demonstrate that our early fusion strategy improves the HOI detection performance.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Guidance from the HOI Detector to VLM</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As explained in Section <a href="#S1" title="1 Introduction ‣ Bilateral Collaboration with Large Vision-Language Models forOpen Vocabulary Human-Object Interaction Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and illustrated in Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ Bilateral Collaboration with Large Vision-Language Models forOpen Vocabulary Human-Object Interaction Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, the features produced by the Vision Tower of BLIP-2 are generalizable but coarse-grained. While the HOI detector is less generalizable, it is better at perceiving fine-grained local features. To combine their advantages, we propose the Attention Bias Guidance (ABG) approach, which guides BLIP-2’s Vision Tower to output instance-level interaction features that are both generalizable and fine-grained. Specifically, ABG approach is based on the cross-attention maps <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{A}_{\text{ho}}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{\text{q}}\times h\times w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mtext>ho</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>q</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{A}_{\text{ho}}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{\text{q}}\times h\times w}</annotation></semantics></math> from the interaction decoder:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\bm{E}_{\text{ho}},\bm{A}_{\text{ho}})=f^{\text{i}}(\bm{M},\bm{E}_{\text{h}}\oplus\bm{E}_{\text{o}})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝑬</mi><mtext>ho</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mtext>ho</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mtext>i</mtext></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑴</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑬</mi><mtext>h</mtext></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝑬</mi><mtext>o</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\bm{E}_{\text{ho}},\bm{A}_{\text{ho}})=f^{\text{i}}(\bm{M},\bm{E}_{\text{h}}\oplus\bm{E}_{\text{o}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\text{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mtext>i</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\text{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the interaction decoder, <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{E}_{\text{h}}\oplus\bm{E}_{\text{o}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑬</mi><mtext>h</mtext></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝑬</mi><mtext>o</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{E}_{\text{h}}\oplus\bm{E}_{\text{o}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the interaction query for <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\text{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mtext>i</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\text{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{E}_{\text{ho}}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{\text{q}}\times D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑬</mi><mtext>ho</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>q</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{E}_{\text{ho}}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{\text{q}}\times D}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the output embeddings of the interaction decoder, and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\oplus" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊕</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\oplus</annotation></semantics></math> stands for element-wise addition. Notably, we only utilize the cross-attention weights relevant to the CNN features in ABG. Therefore, the spatial dimension of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{A}_{\text{ho}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mtext>ho</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{A}_{\text{ho}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\times w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\times w</annotation></semantics></math> instead of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\times w+N_{\text{p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>p</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\times w+N_{\text{p}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since there are multiple cross-attention layers with each layer containing multiple heads in the interaction decoder, we average all cross-attention maps relevant to a HOI query, obtaining its attention weights in <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{A}_{\text{ho}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mtext>ho</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{A}_{\text{ho}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In BLIP-2’s ViT encoder, the <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="cls\_token" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">cls\_token</annotation></semantics></math> (denoted as <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{C}\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑪</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{C}\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> in Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3 Method ‣ Bilateral Collaboration with Large Vision-Language Models forOpen Vocabulary Human-Object Interaction Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) aggregates the features of all patch tokens <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{P}_{\text{img}}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{\text{p}}\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑷</mi><mtext>img</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>p</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{P}_{\text{img}}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{\text{p}}\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>. We duplicate the <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="cls\_token" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">cls\_token</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\text{q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>q</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\text{q}}</annotation></semantics></math> folds to obtain <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{C}_{\text{ho}}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{\text{q}}\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑪</mi><mtext>ho</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>q</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{C}_{\text{ho}}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{\text{q}}\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>, so as to match the number of embeddings in <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{E}_{\text{ho}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑬</mi><mtext>ho</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{E}_{\text{ho}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We freeze all embeddings in <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{C}_{\text{ho}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑪</mi><mtext>ho</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{C}_{\text{ho}}</annotation></semantics></math> and expect each one to focus on the interaction areas of one specified human-object pair. To achieve this, we add each cross-attention map in <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{A}_{\text{ho}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mtext>ho</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{A}_{\text{ho}}</annotation></semantics></math> as bias to the self-attention maps for the corresponding duplicated <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="cls\_token" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">cls\_token</annotation></semantics></math>s in <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{C}_{\text{ho}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑪</mi><mtext>ho</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{C}_{\text{ho}}</annotation></semantics></math>, as illustrated in Figure <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.2 Guidance from the HOI Detector to VLM ‣ 3 Method ‣ Bilateral Collaboration with Large Vision-Language Models forOpen Vocabulary Human-Object Interaction Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>(b). Specifically, each attention map in <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{A}_{\text{ho}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mtext>ho</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{A}_{\text{ho}}</annotation></semantics></math> is firstly up-sampled to a fixed size. Then, it is reshaped to a vector and further adapted by a fully-connected (FC) layer. Finally, the processed attention vector is reshaped back to matrix and added to the self-attention maps of BLIP-2. Moreover, we add attention bias in each self-attention layer of BLIP-2’s ViT encoder.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We adopt the similar strategy to the Q-Former part of BLIP-2. Particularly, the Q-Former contains a set of cross-attention layers and originally employs 32 queries that play similar roles to that of <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="cls\_token" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">cls\_token</annotation></semantics></math> in the ViT encoder. We propose to enrich the input tokens for the Q-Former with the output embeddings of <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{C}_{\text{ho}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑪</mi><mtext>ho</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{C}_{\text{ho}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{P}_{\text{img}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑷</mi><mtext>img</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{P}_{\text{img}}</annotation></semantics></math> from the ViT encoder. Moreover, we increase the number of queries of Q-Former from 32 to <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\text{q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>q</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\text{q}}</annotation></semantics></math>, so as to match the number of HOI queries. This new set of queries is learnable, and denoted as <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{Q}^{\text{q}}_{\text{ho}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝑸</mi><mtext>ho</mtext><mtext>q</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{Q}^{\text{q}}_{\text{ho}}</annotation></semantics></math> in Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3 Method ‣ Bilateral Collaboration with Large Vision-Language Models forOpen Vocabulary Human-Object Interaction Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. Then, we apply the same attention bias as that in the ViT encoder to ensure the output embeddings of Q-Former are also fine-grained. These <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\text{q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>q</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\text{q}}</annotation></semantics></math> output embeddings are denoted as <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{E}^{\text{q}}_{\text{ho}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝑬</mi><mtext>ho</mtext><mtext>q</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{E}^{\text{q}}_{\text{ho}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The whole process to obtain <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{E}^{\text{q}}_{\text{ho}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝑬</mi><mtext>ho</mtext><mtext>q</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{E}^{\text{q}}_{\text{ho}}</annotation></semantics></math> can be represented as follows:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{E}^{\text{q}}_{\text{ho}}=\phi(\bm{P_{\text{img}}},\bm{C},\bm{C}_{\text{ho}};\bm{Q}^{\text{q}}_{\text{ho}}|\bm{A}_{\text{ho}})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑬</mi><mtext>ho</mtext><mtext>q</mtext></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑷</mi><mtext>img</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑪</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝑪</mi><mtext>ho</mtext></msub><mo>;</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑸</mi><mtext>ho</mtext><mtext>q</mtext></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mtext>ho</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{E}^{\text{q}}_{\text{ho}}=\phi(\bm{P_{\text{img}}},\bm{C},\bm{C}_{\text{ho}};\bm{Q}^{\text{q}}_{\text{ho}}|\bm{A}_{\text{ho}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the Vision Tower, <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{C}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{C}_{\text{ho}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑪</mi><mtext>ho</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{C}_{\text{ho}}</annotation></semantics></math> are frozen <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="cls\_token" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">cls\_token</annotation></semantics></math>s, and <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{Q}^{\text{q}}_{\text{ho}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝑸</mi><mtext>ho</mtext><mtext>q</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{Q}^{\text{q}}_{\text{ho}}</annotation></semantics></math> are learnable queries for Q-Former.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we project <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{E}^{\text{q}}_{\text{ho}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝑬</mi><mtext>ho</mtext><mtext>q</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{E}^{\text{q}}_{\text{ho}}</annotation></semantics></math> onto the same feature dimension as <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{E}_{\text{ho}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑬</mi><mtext>ho</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{E}_{\text{ho}}</annotation></semantics></math> using an FC layer and then fuse them via element-wise addition. The fused embeddings can be directly used for interaction classification purposes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fast Computation of ABG.</span> The input tokens for the ViT encoder include <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{C}_{\text{ho}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑪</mi><mtext>ho</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{C}_{\text{ho}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{P}_{\text{img}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑷</mi><mtext>img</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{P}_{\text{img}}</annotation></semantics></math>. However, when they play the role of query in the self-attention operation, they have different receptive fields: the receptive fields for <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{C}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{P}_{\text{img}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑷</mi><mtext>img</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{P}_{\text{img}}</annotation></semantics></math> do not include <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{C}_{\text{ho}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑪</mi><mtext>ho</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{C}_{\text{ho}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the receptive field of each embedding in <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{C}_{\text{ho}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑪</mi><mtext>ho</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{C}_{\text{ho}}</annotation></semantics></math> only include itself and <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{P}_{\text{img}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑷</mi><mtext>img</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{P}_{\text{img}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, the self-attention maps for <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{C}_{\text{ho}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑪</mi><mtext>ho</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{C}_{\text{ho}}</annotation></semantics></math> are influenced by the attention bias from the HOI detector, while those for <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{C}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{P}_{\text{img}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑷</mi><mtext>img</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{P}_{\text{img}}</annotation></semantics></math> are irrelevant to the attention bias. These differences impact our model’s computation efficiency. Therefore, we propose unifying the self-attention operations for <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{C}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{P}_{\text{img}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑷</mi><mtext>img</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{P}_{\text{img}}</annotation></semantics></math> into the ABG framework as <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{C}_{\text{ho}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑪</mi><mtext>ho</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{C}_{\text{ho}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">As illustrated in Figure <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.2 Guidance from the HOI Detector to VLM ‣ 3 Method ‣ Bilateral Collaboration with Large Vision-Language Models forOpen Vocabulary Human-Object Interaction Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>(a), we stack the embeddings for <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{C}_{\text{ho}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑪</mi><mtext>ho</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{C}_{\text{ho}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{P}_{\text{img}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑷</mi><mtext>img</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{P}_{\text{img}}</annotation></semantics></math> together to perform the self-attention operations. Then, we impose different attention bias for different query-key pairs. An attention bias of 0 implies that the attention score for this query-key pair remains unchanged. An attention bias of -<math id="S3.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="inf" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">inf</annotation></semantics></math> indicates that the query-key pair can be disregarded. In other words, the receptive field of this query does not include this key. When the query is one token from <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{C}_{\text{ho}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑪</mi><mtext>ho</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{C}_{\text{ho}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the key is one token in <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{P}_{\text{img}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑷</mi><mtext>img</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{P}_{\text{img}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we adopt the attention bias from the HOI detector. Finally, the above strategy can be extended to the Q-Former, as illustrated in Figure <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.2 Guidance from the HOI Detector to VLM ‣ 3 Method ‣ Bilateral Collaboration with Large Vision-Language Models forOpen Vocabulary Human-Object Interaction Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>(c). Notably, we apply ABG to the cross-attention operations in Q-Former, so the attention maps in Figure <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.2 Guidance from the HOI Detector to VLM ‣ 3 Method ‣ Bilateral Collaboration with Large Vision-Language Models forOpen Vocabulary Human-Object Interaction Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>(c) are not square.
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2507.06510v1/drawing_3.png" id="S3.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:685/347;" width="685" height="347" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="S3.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fast computation of ABG. The <math id="S3.F4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="zero" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">zero</annotation></semantics></math> and -<math id="S3.F4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="inf" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">inf</annotation></semantics></math> matrices denote those with elements that are <math id="S3.F4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> and -<math id="S3.F4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="inf" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">inf</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. Diagonal elements in the ‘mask matrix’ are <math id="S3.F4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> and the others are -<math id="S3.F4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="inf" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">inf</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Guidance from VLM to the HOI Detector</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The ABG approach relies on the attention maps produced by the HOI detector. In the following, we propose an LLM-based Supervision Guidance (LSG) approach to enhance the quality of these attention maps. This approach works by requiring the LLM component of BLIP-2 to generate more fine-grained and interaction-relevant captions according to the attention bias provided by ABG.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Specifically, as illustrated in Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3 Method ‣ Bilateral Collaboration with Large Vision-Language Models forOpen Vocabulary Human-Object Interaction Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we add another set of queries <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{Q}_{\text{f}}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{\text{f}}\times D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑸</mi><mtext>f</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>f</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{Q}_{\text{f}}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{\text{f}}\times D}</annotation></semantics></math> to the detection decoder of the HOI detector, a set of duplicated frozen <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="cls\_token" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">cls\_token</annotation></semantics></math>s <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{C}_{\text{f}}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{\text{f}}\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑪</mi><mtext>f</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>f</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{C}_{\text{f}}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{\text{f}}\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> to the ViT encoder of BLIP-2, and a set of <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\text{f}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>f</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\text{f}}</annotation></semantics></math> learnable queries <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{Q}^{\text{q}}_{\text{f}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝑸</mi><mtext>f</mtext><mtext>q</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{Q}^{\text{q}}_{\text{f}}</annotation></semantics></math> to the Q-Former of BLIP-2. The output embeddings for <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{Q}_{\text{f}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑸</mi><mtext>f</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{Q}_{\text{f}}</annotation></semantics></math> from the detection decoder are denoted as <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{E}_{\text{f}}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{\text{f}}\times D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑬</mi><mtext>f</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>f</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{E}_{\text{f}}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{\text{f}}\times D}</annotation></semantics></math>, which are further utilized as the query for the interaction decoder. Based on the ABG approach, we obtain the cross-attention maps of <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{E}_{\text{f}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑬</mi><mtext>f</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{E}_{\text{f}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which are denoted as <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{A}_{\text{f}}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{\text{f}}\times h\times w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mtext>f</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>f</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{A}_{\text{f}}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{\text{f}}\times h\times w}</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, with the attention bias contained in <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{A}_{\text{f}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mtext>f</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{A}_{\text{f}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain a set of <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\text{f}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>f</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\text{f}}</annotation></semantics></math> embeddings <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{E}^{\text{q}}_{\text{f}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝑬</mi><mtext>f</mtext><mtext>q</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{E}^{\text{q}}_{\text{f}}</annotation></semantics></math> from the Q-Former of BLIP-2. Fast computation of ABG in this step is illustrated in Figure <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.2 Guidance from the HOI Detector to VLM ‣ 3 Method ‣ Bilateral Collaboration with Large Vision-Language Models forOpen Vocabulary Human-Object Interaction Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>(c).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Then, we feed the embeddings in <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{E}^{\text{q}}_{\text{f}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝑬</mi><mtext>f</mtext><mtext>q</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{E}^{\text{q}}_{\text{f}}</annotation></semantics></math> as tokens to the LLM, prompting it to produce fine-grained captions in the auto-regressive manner <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">76</a>]</cite>. In our approach, we obtain the ‘ground-truth’ captions via a state-of-the-art MLLM (e.g., GPT-4o <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib77" title="" class="ltx_ref">77</a>]</cite>). Specially, we prompt the MLLM to produce captions that contain object locality information (e.g., “on the left” and “on the bottom”) to match the nature of the detection tasks. More details regarding the captioning process are provided in the supplementary material. Moreover, since the HOI labels contain verbs and nouns only, we apply different weights on the predicted tokens according to the part-of-speech:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle L_{\text{lsg}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>lsg</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle L_{\text{lsg}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{\gamma}{N_{\text{t}}}{\sum^{N_{\text{t}}}_{n=1}w_{n}*\text{CELoss}(\bm{t}_{n},\bm{g}_{n})}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>γ</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>t</mtext></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>t</mtext></msub></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mtext>CELoss</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒈</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{\gamma}{N_{\text{t}}}{\sum^{N_{\text{t}}}_{n=1}w_{n}*\text{CELoss}(\bm{t}_{n},\bm{g}_{n})},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bm{t}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bm{t}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=f^{\text{opt}}(\bm{E}^{\text{q}}_{\text{f}}|\bm{g}_{1},\bm{g}_{2},...,\bm{g}_{n-1})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑬</mi><mtext>f</mtext><mtext>q</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒈</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒈</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒈</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=f^{\text{opt}}(\bm{E}^{\text{q}}_{\text{f}}|\bm{g}_{1},\bm{g}_{2},...,\bm{g}_{n-1}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{lsg}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>lsg</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{lsg}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes our modified auto-regressive loss in BLIP-2, and <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{N_{\text{t}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>t</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{N_{\text{t}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of valid tokens. In addition, <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\text{opt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\text{opt}}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the LLM component (OPT-2.7B) of BLIP-2. <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{t}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒕</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{t}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{g}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒈</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{g}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the loss weight, token weight, predicted logits, and the ground-truth one-hot label vector for the <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-th token, respectively. Besides, the value of <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> for noun, verb, and all the other tokens are set to <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>, and 1.0, respectively.
Since we freeze all model parameters of BLIP-2 except for <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{Q}^{\text{q}}_{\text{f}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝑸</mi><mtext>f</mtext><mtext>q</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{Q}^{\text{q}}_{\text{f}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the gradients produced by <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{lsg}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>lsg</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{lsg}}</annotation></semantics></math> mainly influence the optimization of the HOI detector. Therefore, our LSG approach drives the HOI detector to produce attention maps that cover a broader range of semantic concepts, allowing the LLM to generate fine-grained captions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, we enhance the quality of the interaction representations (i.e., <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{E}_{\text{ho}}\oplus\bm{E}^{\text{q}}_{\text{ho}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑬</mi><mtext>ho</mtext></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑬</mi><mtext>ho</mtext><mtext>q</mtext></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{E}_{\text{ho}}\oplus\bm{E}^{\text{q}}_{\text{ho}}</annotation></semantics></math>) by applying the cross-attention with <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{E}^{\text{q}}_{\text{f}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝑬</mi><mtext>f</mtext><mtext>q</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{E}^{\text{q}}_{\text{f}}</annotation></semantics></math> using a single transformer layer. We refer to this layer as ‘Fusion Adapter’ in Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3 Method ‣ Bilateral Collaboration with Large Vision-Language Models forOpen Vocabulary Human-Object Interaction Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. Finally, we adopt the same loss functions for object detection and interaction prediction as those in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, and we denote these loss functions as <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{hoi}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>hoi</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{hoi}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Following existing works, we utilize BLIP-2’s text encoder to construct interaction classifiers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, so as to match the open vocabulary interaction recognition setting. Thus, the total loss function of our model can be represented as follows:</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=L_{\text{hoi}}+L_{\text{lsg}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>hoi</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>lsg</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=L_{\text{hoi}}+L_{\text{lsg}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Discussion.</span> Multiple studies also employ extra image captions to enhance the generalization ability of HOI detectors. However, they typically extract HOI triplets as pseudo labels from the captions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>, <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite> or impose coarse supervision signals (e.g., knowledge distillation using the holistic textual feature extracted from a text encoder <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib78" title="" class="ltx_ref">78</a>]</cite>). In comparison, we propose to adopt the LLM and auto-regressive loss to impose token-level fine-grained supervisions and also avoid the complicated pseudo-labeling process.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Implementation Details</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate the performance of our model on two popular benchmarks (i.e., HICO-DET <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite> and V-COCO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>). We adopt the AdamW optimizer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite> and conduct experiments with a batch size of 16 on eight 3090 GPUs. We set <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{f}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> to 64, 32, 1.5, 2, and 0.1, respectively. The initial learning rate is set to <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math> and then multiplied by 0.1 after 40 epochs. We initialize model parameters according to the weights of the DETR model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite> that is pre-trained on MS-COCO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">79</a>]</cite>. For the other implementation details, we basically follow those in UniHOI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> to facilitate fair comparisons with recent works.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Comparisons in the Open Vocabulary Setting</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare the performance of our approach with state-of-the-art methods in four open vocabulary evaluation settings, following GEN-VLKT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>. The experimental results are summarized in Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.2 Comparisons in the Open Vocabulary Setting ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Bilateral Collaboration with Large Vision-Language Models forOpen Vocabulary Human-Object Interaction Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Unseen Composition (UC).</span> In this setting, the same objects and verbs appear in the training and testing stages. However, some verb-object combinations that are unseen in the training stage appear in the testing stage. The UC setting includes two sub-settings (i.e., RF-UC and NF-UC), which indicate that a part of the rare and non-rare verb-object combinations are unseen during training, respectively. In the RF-UC setting, our method outperforms the second-best approach by 11.82% and 4.50% mAP on the unseen and seen HOI categories, respectively. In the NF-UC setting, our method outperforms the second-best method by 4.14% and 4.61% mAP on the unseen and seen HOI categories, respectively. Our method’s improved performance on the unseen HOI categories is significant. These results indicate that our ABG approach provides accurate attention bias even for unseen verb-object combinations, which enables the VLM to extract generalizable interaction representations and compensates for the lack of training data for these combinations.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="S4.T1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Performance comparisons on HICO-DET in the open vocabulary settings. <span id="S4.T1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bold</span> represents the best performance, and <span id="S4.T1.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">underline</span> indicates the second-best performance.</span></figcaption>
<div id="S4.T1.7" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:208.7pt;height:122.7pt;vertical-align:-60.2pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-122.2pt,71.8pt) scale(0.460630965527673,0.460630965527673) ;">
